## 3. Low-center phase capacity is monotone on the whole enlarged interval

Assume the larger leaf has mass at least

\[
U_0=0.525.
\]

The low-center branch ends at

\[
C_0=W_0-2U_0=0.862606.
\]

The candidate uses

\[
\Phi(C)
=C(W_0-C)
-{1\over10}\left(3-{W_0\over C}\right)
\left({W_0+C\over2}-K\right)^2,
\qquad K={2\over\sqrt3}.
\]

A potential audit failure would be checking only `Phi'(C_0)>0` while allowing an
interior maximum.  This does not occur.  Put

\[
r={W_0+C\over2}-K,
\qquad
q=3-{W_0\over C}.
\]

Then

\[
\Phi''(C)
=-2-{1\over10}
\left(
-{2W_0r^2\over C^3}
+{2W_0r\over C^2}
+{q\over2}
\right).
\]

On the whole relevant interval `0.525<C<=0.862606`, one has `0<r<0.234` and
`q>-0.644`.  Dropping the positive middle term gives a crude lower bound on the
parenthesis greater than `-2`, hence

\[
\Phi''(C)<-1.8<0.
\]

Therefore `Phi'` is decreasing and its minimum is at `C_0`.  The exact certificate
checks

\[
\Phi'(C_0)>0.155
\]

and

\[
E_0-\Phi(C_0)>0.005409.
\]

Thus the entire low-center interval is excluded, not merely its endpoint.

**Verdict:** PASS.

